The '''Kuvari War''' (1796–1801) was a conflict in the [[Bolodun and Kuvari Islands]] between the [[Capuyaquiran League]] and the [[Kuvari people|Kuvari]] insurgency. It arose from the social upheaval that followed the emancipation of enslaved Sudanese Kaftians and their relocation to the island of [[Bolodun]], where they came into conflict with the Kuvari population. The war ended with the killing of large numbers of Kuvari on Bolodun and the expulsion of the survivors to [[Tumala]] and the outer islands, a division that defined the islands' politics for the next two centuries.

== Background ==

The Bolodun and Kuvari Islands had been a Burawi plantation colony worked by enslaved labour, and sugar production made Bolodun the principal island of the group. Following the [[Abolition of Slavery Act]] in [[Burawa]] in 1789, which also ended Burawi trade with states that traded in slaves, the League banned slavery the same year. The subsequent [[Emancipation of Slaves Act (Capuyaquiran League)|Emancipation of Slaves Act]] ordered that emancipated slaves who did not sign contracts to work on existing farms be relocated to Bolodun, which was granted autonomous control under the leader [[Iacob Kheme]].

The arrival of freed Sudanese Kaftians on Bolodun, where the Kuvari population had lived under an oppressive social hierarchy, produced an era of civil unrest. The Kuvari of Bolodun and the outer islands resisted the new order, and the conflict escalated into an organised insurgency. The League, whose other islands shared the Burawi colonial social structure, provided military support to the Sudanese Kaftian administration.

== Course of the war ==

The war was fought between League-supported forces and the Kuvari insurgents between 1796 and 1801. The insurgents drew support from Kuvari communities across the islands, while the League's military backing gave the Bolodun administration overwhelming force. By the closing years of the war the conflict had taken on the character of a campaign against the Kuvari population itself rather than against the insurgency as an organised force.

== Aftermath ==

The war culminated in the killing of large numbers of Kuvari on Bolodun and the expulsion of the survivors to Tumala and the other islands. The genocide and expulsion destroyed the Kuvari presence on the island from which they derived their historic name, leaving Bolodun under a Sudanese Kaftian administration and the outer islands under Kuvari leadership, led by [[Mariba Kuna]].

The islands' two administrations — one on Bolodun run by Sudanese Kaftians and one on Tumala led by the Kuvari — remained generally unfriendly toward one another in the aftermath of the war. The division shaped the islands' relationship with the rest of the League for the next two centuries, and contributed to a rapprochement between the two island communities as both were cast aside by the other League members, whose communities treated the Bolodun and Kuvari islands with racism and contempt.

